# Altitude — line-ending contract.
#
# The repo had none until 2026-07-28, so what landed in a working tree depended
# on each contributor's `core.autocrlf`. Every tracked text blob in the repo is
# already LF (verified with `git cat-file blob`), so this file changes nothing
# about what git STORES — only what lands on disk.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Default: LF in the working tree on EVERY platform.
#
# The first version of this file said `* text=auto` and left the working tree
# platform-native, on the reasoning that it "does not force a re-checkout".
# That conservatism has a measured price, so it is reversed here.
#
# THE MEASUREMENT (2026-07-28, same commit, Node 20.18.1 + pnpm 9.15.0 on both
# sides — a Windows CRLF working tree vs. a real Linux build of the same tree):
#
#   Windows dist total   3,563,072 B
#   Linux   dist total   3,524,525 B
#   ---------------------------------
#   difference              38,547 B  (1.094 %)
#
#   875 of 1022 built files were byte-identical; 147 differed; 0 files existed
#   on only one platform. Every byte of the 38,547 is a carriage return:
#     - 18,959 CRLF line ends inside `sourcesContent` in Vite's .js.map files,
#       serialized as the escaped `\r` sequence — 2 bytes each = 37,918 B. The
#       maps embed the .ts sources verbatim, so a CRLF checkout is copied
#       straight into the build output.
#     -    629 raw CR bytes in the .svg / .hbs / .js assets that
#       `scripts/copy-assets-to-dist.js` mirrors from source into dist/.
#
# Why that matters: `.github/workflows/v2-checks.yml` (`baselines-bundle`)
# allows 1 % drift. The EOL delta ALONE is 1.094 %, so with a native-EOL
# checkout `.altitude/baselines/bundle/snapshot.json` could only ever be
# captured on Linux, and a Windows capture was guaranteed to fail CI in the
# opposite direction. Pinning the working tree to LF makes the build
# byte-reproducible across platforms and the gate satisfiable from any OS.
#
# The repo has no `.bat`/`.cmd`/`.ps1` files, which are the only common case
# that genuinely needs CRLF; add an explicit override here if one is ever
# introduced.
#
# Applying this to an existing clone (git does not re-checkout on its own):
#
#   git add .gitattributes && git commit -m 'eol'   # attributes must be committed FIRST
#   git rm --cached -r . -q && git reset --hard     # or: git checkout-index -a -f
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* text=auto eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Generated artifacts.
#
# Now covered by the global `eol=lf` above, but kept listed because these are
# the files where a native-EOL checkout was actively harmful and the reason is
# worth keeping next to the paths: their emitters write LF unconditionally
# (`JSON.stringify`, `lines.join('\n')`).
# With a native-EOL checkout the file on disk is CRLF, so every regeneration
# rewrote it as LF.
# The content hash is unchanged, but git's stat cache can never settle
# (`git status` shows " M" forever while `git diff` is empty — see
# `git status --porcelain=v2`, which prints the same blob hash on both sides).
# Pinning the checkout to LF makes generate → status a genuine no-op.
#
# This does NOT fix carriage returns that end up inside JSON *string values*
# (serialized as the escaped `\r` sequence). Those are file content, not line
# terminators, and git normalization cannot touch them — they are fixed in the
# emitters: `libs/al-web-components/cem-plugins/al-deterministic.mjs` and
# `scripts/generate-component-schemas.js`.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
libs/al-web-components/custom-elements.json                text eol=lf
libs/al-web-components/schemas/**                          text eol=lf
libs/al-web-components/components/icon/fonts/iconfont.css  text eol=lf

# Phosphor icon system — everything below is written by
# `libs/al-web-components/icons/icons-config.mjs` (`pnpm --filter
# @southleft/al-web-components build:icons`) with `join('\n')`, and all of it is COMMITTED
# (1,512 glyph modules + 37 legacy shims + five barrels). That combination is
# what makes it EOL-sensitive: a CRLF checkout means every `build:icons` run
# rewrites ~1,560 tracked files as LF, which is both the worst possible
# `git status` noise and enough churn to move the bundle baseline.
#
# The `iconfont.svg` entry that used to sit above is gone with the webfont.
libs/al-web-components/components/icon/phosphor/**         text eol=lf
libs/al-web-components/components/icon/icons/**            text eol=lf
libs/al-web-components/components/icon/glyphs.ts           text eol=lf
libs/al-web-components/components/icon/catalog.ts          text eol=lf
libs/al-web-components/components/icon/all.ts              text eol=lf
libs/al-web-components/components/icon/lazy.ts             text eol=lf
libs/al-web-components/components/icon/icon-aliases.ts     text eol=lf
.altitude/baselines/**/*.json                              text eol=lf
.altitude/ai-readiness/**/*.json                           text eol=lf
.altitude/golden-snapshots/*.expected                      text eol=lf

# Shell scripts must keep LF or they will not execute on Linux/macOS/CI.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Lockfiles are tool-written; keep them byte-stable across platforms.
pnpm-lock.yaml text eol=lf
package-lock.json text eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Binary — never EOL-convert, never diff as text.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.woff2 binary
*.woff  binary
*.ttf   binary
*.eot   binary
*.otf   binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.webp  binary
*.pdf   binary
*.zip   binary
